To determine if an octagon with all right angles, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in any $n$-sided polygon is $180(n-2)$ degrees, and the sum of the angles of a polygon is equal to the sum of the Exterior Angles, so we have:
$$ 360\cdot (n - 2) = \angle^{\circ}_{1}+\angle^{\circ}_{2}+\angle^{\circ}_{3}+\cdots+\angle^{\circ}_{n}=\angle_{1}+\angle_{2}+\angle_{3}+\cdots+\angle_{n} $$

In an $n$-sided polygon, the sum of the exterior angles is always $360^\circ$. We can think of each of the five exterior angles of a pentagon and each of the six exterior angles of a hexagon as being formed on one of the eight sides of an octagon, which will obviously sum to $360^\circ$. Subsituting $540^\circ$ for the sum of the octagon's interior angles, we have:
$$ 360\cdot (n - 2) = 540\cdot (n - 2+1) = 360\cdot (n-2)\cdot 2 = 360\cdot (n-2). $$

